{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HJFzEAn4zJH2piRQzyc763","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016svwBNrGVuBL2TXJUFm1dY","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus area is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Output-Distribution Distillation Discovers Hidden Neuron Importance in SwiGLU Activation Sparsity\"\n\n**Core idea:** Training SwiGLU sparsity predictors with KL-divergence loss (distilling the dense model's output distribution) instead of the standard BCE loss (against magnitude-based oracle masks) yields fundamentally different sparsity masks that diverge 53% from magnitude-based oracles. A causal ablation shows that the mask patterns themselves (not the gradient coupling during training) are the sole driver of KL's advantage: a BCE predictor trained on KL-derived targets matches KL performance exactly (PPL delta ≤0.03, downstream delta <1pp across two seeds).\n\n**Model:** Llama 3.1 8B. Sparsity predictors are lightweight 2-layer MLPs trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-2, sequence length 2048.\n\n**Key claims:**\n1. KL distillation reveals neuron importance patterns diverging 53% from magnitude-based oracles; causal ablation confirms these patterns (not gradient coupling) drive quality advantage.\n2. A 6-point sparsity regime curve (25–50%) identifies three operating regimes: budget-surplus (≤30%), budget-constrained (35–45%), capacity-cliff (≥50%).\n3. Comprehensive evaluation across PPL, 4 downstream tasks, LongBench (4K–32K), MT-Bench, and RULER.\n\n**Method details (from paper pages 3-4):**\n- BCE baseline: L_BCE = standard binary cross-entropy against magnitude oracle masks t_j = 1[|a_j| > τ]\n- KL distillation: L_KL = KL(P_dense || P_sparse(m)), gradient couples all neurons through Jacobian\n- Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation for discrete mask (τ_gs annealed 1.0→0.1)\n- Lagrangian sparsity control: L = L_task + λ(mean mask - (1-s_target))\n- Gradient coupling analysis: BCE gradient is per-neuron; KL gradient propagates through full output distribution\n\n**Experimental results:**\n\nTable 1 (30% sparsity): Dense PPL=6.13, TEAL=10.97, BCE forced top-k=11.24, KL uniform=8.82, KL global=8.98.\n\nTable 2 (C1 causal ablation at 50% uniform): KL predictor vs BCE+KL-targets across seeds s42 and s123. PPL deltas = 0.00 and 0.03. Max downstream delta = 0.79pp.\n\nTable 3 (6-point regime curve, 25–50%): Strictly monotonic. Three regimes identified. Uniform outperforms global at low sparsity, global better at moderate, irrelevant at high.\n\nTable 4 (LongBench): KL 30% retains 80.9% of dense, TEAL 30% retains 66.7%.\n\nTable 5 (MT-Bench): KL 30% retains 92.0% (3.82/4.15), TEAL 30% retains 79.0% (3.28/4.15). Only KL 30% per-category scores shown; Dense and TEAL per-category missing.\n\nTable 6 (RULER 4K/16K): KL 30% advantage most pronounced on multi-key retrieval (+13.3pp over TEAL at 4K).\n\nDiscussion: 14B scale caveat (PPL advantage preserved, MMLU doesn't generalize). Iso-compute control (BCE with 2.71× more FLOPs still PPL 27.97 vs KL 12.00).\n\n**Review context (imagined experiments from plan):**\nThe review context lists these imagined experiments: C1 causal ablation (covered), 6-point regime curve (covered), BCE 30% forced top-k (covered in Table 1), LongBench (covered), MT-Bench (partially covered—missing Dense/TEAL per-category), RULER (covered).\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the method well-motivated and correctly formulated? Are there technical issues?\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: Are experiments well-designed? Are baselines fair? Are there confounds? Statistical significance?\n3. **Causal Ablation Design**: Is the ablation convincing? Are there alternative explanations not ruled out?\n4. **Missing Experiments / Controls**: What additional experiments would strengthen or are needed for the claims?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n6. **Clarity of Presentation**: Are the methods and results clearly presented?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors**\n- **Missing Experiments**\n- **Preliminary Score** (1-6) with justification\n- **Confidence** (1-5)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19310,"cache_read_input_tokens":25056,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19310},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d20b1017-1e16-43f7-9487-d4eda4cc7ade","uuid":"44c164fb-b071-4d50-ac53-9b6980de5b94"}
