relaxation for discrete mask (τ_gs annealed 1.0→0.1)\n- Lagrangian sparsity control: L = L_task + λ(mean mask - (1-s_target))\n- Gradient coupling analysis: BCE gradient is per-neuron; KL gradient propagates through full output distribution\n\n**Experimental results:**\n\nTable 1 (30% sparsity): Dense PPL=6.13, TEAL=10.97, BCE forced top-k=11.24, KL uniform=8.82, KL global=8.98.\n\nTable 2 (C1 causal ablation at 50% uniform): KL predictor vs BCE+KL-targets across seeds s42 and s123. PPL deltas = 0.00 and 0.03. Max downstream delta = 0.79pp.\n\nTable 3 (6-point regime curve, 25–50%): Strictly monotonic. Three regimes identified. Uniform outperforms global at low sparsity, global better at moderate, irrelevant at high.\n\nTable 4 (LongBench): KL 30% retains 80.9% of dense, TEAL 30% retains 66.7%.\n\nTable 5 (MT-Bench): KL 30% retains 92.0% (3.82/4.15), TEAL 30% retains 79.0% (3.28/4.15). Only KL 30% per-category scores shown; Dense and TEAL per-category missing.\n\nTable 6 (RULER 4K/16K): KL 30% advantage most pronounced on multi-key retrieval (+13.3pp over TEAL at 4K).\n\nDiscussion: 14B scale caveat (PPL advantage preserved, MMLU doesn't generalize). Iso-compute control (BCE with 2.71× more FLOPs still PPL 27.97 vs KL 12.00).\n\n**Review context (imagined experiments from plan):**\nThe review context lists these imagined experiments: C1 causal ablation (covered), 6-point regime curve (covered), BCE 30% forced top-k (covered in Table 1), LongBench (covered), MT-Bench (partially covered—missing Dense/TEAL per-category), RULER (covered).\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the method well-motivated and correctly formulated? Are there technical issues?\n2. **Experimental Rigor**: Are experiments well-designed? Are baselines fair? Are there confounds? Statistical significance?\n3. **Causal Ablation Design**: Is the ablation convincing? Are there alternative explanations not ruled out?\n4. **Missing Experiments 