{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015tv4uUQBeWg5UX9j8PpeMR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section*{Limitations}\n2\t\n3\tOur primary evaluation uses WikiText-2 perplexity, and downstream benchmarks reveal that perplexity is a poor proxy for reasoning ability at moderate sparsity: 40\\% sparsity achieves PPL 8.36 but MMLU already degrades, motivating 30\\% as the recommended operating point for downstream fidelity.\n4\tTraining requires 1000 steps on WikiText-103 ($\\sim$45 minutes on RTX PRO 6000 for KL, $\\sim$17 minutes for BCE), but a separate predictor must be trained for each sparsity target.\n5\tOur recommended operating point (30\\% sparsity, uniform allocation) is validated at 7--8B scale; at 14B, the MMLU gap persists regardless of allocation strategy (\\S\\ref{sec:14b}). Scaling to 70B remains untested.\n6\tOut-of-distribution evaluation (Appendix~\\ref{app:ood}) covers 30\\% and 50\\% sparsity on C4; extending to 70\\% is left to future work.\n7\tThe causal ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:mask_analysis}) confirms that mask target quality, not gradient coupling, is the primary driver of KL's advantage. However, the mechanism by which KL's coupled gradient discovers superior mask patterns during training remains incompletely characterized; future work could trace per-layer mask divergence trajectories across training to identify when and where the patterns diverge.\n8\tAt 30\\% sparsity, global allocation exhibits 9.5pp cross-seed MMLU standard deviation (3-seed); allocation strategy interacts with predictor initialization in task-specific ways. This variance is MMLU-specific, as ARC, WinoGrande, and GSM8K show stable performance across seeds under both allocation strategies.\n9\tWhile we motivate our work with inference speedup potential, actual wall-clock acceleration depends on sparse kernel implementations (e.g., Triton, cuSPARSE) and hardware-specific memory access patterns; this work focuses on prediction quality rather than systems optimization.\n10\tFuture directions include N:M structured sparsity for hardware-native acceleration, scaling to 13B+ models, and training a single predictor that serves multiple sparsity targets simultaneously.\n11\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016svwBNrGVuBL2TXJUFm1dY","session_id":"d20b1017-1e16-43f7-9487-d4eda4cc7ade","uuid":"2b8d6360-6fb9-48d4-bf53-7d5823e916ec","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:25:01.739Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HJFzEAn4zJH2piRQzyc763","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UJYsqRurXhfRN8kEb1PKm9","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Output-Distribution Distillation Discovers Hidden Neuron Importance in SwiGLU Activation Sparsity\"\n\n**Core idea:** Training SwiGLU sparsity predictors with KL-divergence loss (distilling the dense model's output distribution) instead of the standard BCE loss (against magnitude-based oracle masks) yields fundamentally different sparsity masks that diverge 53% from magnitude-based oracles. A causal ablation confirms that the discovered mask patterns (not gradient coupling) are the sole driver of KL's advantage.\n\n**Model:** Llama 3.1 8B. Predictors are 2-layer MLPs trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-2 (seq len 2048).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Empirical discovery that KL-divergence distillation reveals neuron importance patterns diverging 53% from magnitude-based oracles, with causal ablation evidence.\n2. 6-point sparsity regime curve identifying three operating regimes with deployment guidelines.\n3. Comprehensive evaluation across PPL, downstream tasks, LongBench, MT-Bench, RULER.\n\n**Related Work in paper (Section 2):**\n- **Training-Free Activation Sparsity**: WINA [1], TEAL [8], R-Sparse [11] — magnitude thresholding, zero training cost, quality gap at moderate sparsity.\n- **Static Learned Corrections**: SPON [3] — static bias correction via KL distillation, input-independent. Paper claims SPON's correction is near-orthogonal to KL's contextual mask effect (cosine similarity 0.196).\n- **Contextual Learned Predictors**: FastForward [10] — BCE predictor + compensation MLP (67.2M params). Paper claims KL masks eliminate need for compensation network.\n- **Knowledge Distillation for Efficiency**: [2, 7] — standard KD. Paper distinguishes by distilling into masking decisions rather than model compression.\n\n**References (11 total):**\n[1] Alizadeh et al. 2024 (LLM in a Flash)\n[2] Hinton et al. 2015 (KD)\n[3] Huang et al. 2024 (SPON)\n[4] Lee et al. 2024 (CATS)\n[5] Liu et al. 2023 (Deja Vu)\n[6] Raposo et al. 2024 (Mixture-of-Depths)\n[7] Sanh et al. 2019 (DistilBERT)\n[8] Song et al. 2024 (TEAL)\n[9] Song et al. 2024 (ProSparse)\n[10] Sun et al. 2024 (FastForward)\n[11] Zhang et al. 2024 (R-Sparse)\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- KL uniform at 30%: PPL 8.82 vs TEAL 10.97 (−19.6%)\n- BCE forced top-k at 30%: PPL 11.24 (worse than TEAL)\n- Causal ablation: BCE+KL-targets matches KL exactly (PPL delta ≤0.03)\n- 14B caveat: PPL advantage preserved but MMLU doesn't generalize\n- Iso-compute: BCE with 2.71× FLOPs still PPL 27.97 vs KL 12.00\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is this contribution? Is \"replacing BCE with KL for sparsity prediction\" a sufficient contribution? Is the causal ablation finding genuinely surprising?\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are there missing related works? Is the positioning accurate? Are 11 references sufficient?\n3. **Claim Calibration**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is \"discovers hidden neuron importance\" overselling?\n4. **Positioning vs. Prior Work**: Is the distinction from SPON (which also uses KL) clear? From FastForward?\n5. **Broader Impact / Significance**: Will this change how the community thinks about sparsity prediction?\n6. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Any issues with presentation, AI-writing artifacts?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors**\n- **Missing Related Work**\n- **Preliminary Score** (1-6) with justification\n- **Confidence** (1-5)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19310,"cache_read_input_tokens":25056,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":19310},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d20b1017-1e16-43f7-9487-d4eda4cc7ade","uuid":"b28f9c38-9f61-4098-9955-548bf9d45f44"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a4349f4f5dbcc308e","tool_use_id":"toolu_01UJYsqRurXhfRN8kEb1PKm9","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus area is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** \"Output-Distribution Distillation Discovers Hidden Neuron Importance in SwiGLU Activation Sparsity\"\n\n**Core idea:** Training SwiGLU sparsity predictors with KL-divergence loss (distilling the dense model's output distribution) instead of the standard BCE loss (against magnitude-based oracle masks) yields fundamentally different sparsity masks that diverge 53% from magnitude-based oracles. A causal ablation confirms that the discovered mask patterns (not gradient coupling) are the sole driver of KL's advantage.\n\n**Model:** Llama 3.1 8B. Predictors are 2-layer MLPs trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-2 (seq len 2048).\n\n**Contributions claimed:**\n1. Empirical discovery that KL-divergence distillation reveals neuron importance patterns diverging 53% from magnitude-based oracles, with causal ablation evidence.\n2. 6-point sparsity regime curve identifying three operating regimes with deployment guidelines.\n3. Comprehensive evaluation across PPL, downstream tasks, LongBench, MT-Bench, RULER.\n\n**Related Work in paper (Section 2):**\n- **Training-Free Activation Sparsity**: WINA [1], TEAL [8], R-Sparse [11] — magnitude thresholding, zero training cost, quality gap at moderate sparsity.\n- **Static Learned Corrections**: SPON [3] — static bias correction via KL distillation, input-independent. Paper claims SPON's correction is near-orthogonal to KL's contextual mask effect (cosine similarity 0.196).\n- **Contextual Learned Predictors**: FastForward [10] — BCE predictor + compensation MLP (67.2M params). Paper claims KL masks eliminate need for compensation network.\n- **Knowledge Distillation for Efficiency**: [2, 7] — standard KD. Paper distinguishes by distilling into masking decisions rather than model compression.\n\n**References (11 total):**\n[1] Alizadeh et al. 2024 (LLM in a Flash)\n[2] Hinton et al. 2015 (KD)\n[3] Huang et al. 2024 (SPON)\n[4] Lee et al. 2024 (CATS)\n[5] Liu et al. 2023 (Deja Vu)\n[6] Raposo et al. 2024 (Mixture-of-Depths)\n[7] Sanh et al. 2019 (DistilBERT)\n[8] Song et al. 2024 (TEAL)\n[9] Song et al. 2024 (ProSparse)\n[10] Sun et al. 2024 (FastForward)\n[11] Zhang et al. 2024 (R-Sparse)\n\n**Key experimental results:**\n- KL uniform at 30%: PPL 8.82 vs TEAL 10.97 (−19.6%)\n- BCE forced top-k at 30%: PPL 11.24 (worse than TEAL)\n- Causal ablation: BCE+KL-targets matches KL exactly (PPL delta ≤0.03)\n- 14B caveat: PPL advantage preserved but MMLU doesn't generalize\n- Iso-compute: BCE with 2.71× FLOPs still PPL 27.97 vs KL 12.00\n\n## Your Task\n\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment**: How novel is this contribution? Is \"replacing BCE with KL for sparsity prediction\" a sufficient contribution? Is the causal ablation finding genuinely surprising?\n2. **Related Work Completeness**: Are there missing related works? Is the positioning accurate? Are 11 references sufficient?\n3. **Claim Calibration**: Are the claims appropriately scoped? Is \"discovers hidden neuron importance\" overselling?\n4. **Positioning vs. Prior Work**: Is the distinction from SPON (which also uses KL) clear? From FastForward?\n5. **Broader Impact / Significance**: Will this change how the community thinks about sparsity prediction?\n6. **Writing Quality**: Is the paper well-written? Any issues with presentation, AI-writing artifacts?\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted, specific)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, specific, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors**\n- **Missing Related Work**\n- **Preliminary Score** (1-6) with justification\n- **Confidence** (1-5)\n","uuid":"e60d4621-6911-48f1-8da3-7eb2d2e3d44f","session_id":"d20b1017-1e16-43f7-9487-d4eda4cc7ade"}
