{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0141BXsgvxmXa67jUiWrzYge","type":"tool_result","content":"paper/experiments.tex:36:\\begin{table}[t]\npaper/experiments.tex:38:[Omitted long matching line]\npaper/experiments.tex:92:\\begin{table}[t]\npaper/experiments.tex:94:\\caption{Ablation at 50\\% sparsity on LLaMA-3.1-8B (WikiText-2 PPL$\\downarrow$, unified benchmark protocol).}\npaper/experiments.tex:144:\\caption{Perplexity scaling with sparsity. The KL predictor's advantage grows disproportionately as more neurons are pruned.}\npaper/experiments.tex:148:\\begin{table}[t]\npaper/experiments.tex:150:\\caption{PPL scaling with sparsity (WikiText-2). KL advantage grows disproportionately with sparsity.}\npaper/experiments.tex:170:\\begin{table}[t]\npaper/experiments.tex:172:\\caption{Allocation analysis: global (TEAL) vs.\\ uniform per-layer allocation across sparsity levels. Entries with $\\pm$ report 3-seed mean$\\pm$std; remaining entries use seed 42. Each row uses a predictor trained at the respective sparsity target.}\npaper/method.tex:159:\\begin{table}[t]\npaper/method.tex:161:\\caption{Taxonomy of learned sparsity prediction methods along correction granularity (rows) and training signal coupling (columns). Our method fills the previously unexplored Per-token $\\times$ Coupled cell.}\npaper/method.tex:191:\\caption{Method overview: a lightweight per-layer predictor produces importance scores trained via KL divergence with Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation. At inference, scores feed TEAL's global top-$k$ allocation without compensation networks.}\npaper/appendix.tex:8:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:10:\\caption{Inference latency on LLaMA-3.1-8B (RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell, batch=1, seq=2048, fp16, 100 forward passes). Overhead is relative to dense inference.}\npaper/appendix.tex:42:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:44:\\caption{Predictor bottleneck sensitivity at 50\\% sparsity (WikiText-2 PPL$\\downarrow$). TEAL global is the primary inference protocol; per-token shown for reference.}\npaper/appendix.tex:66:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:68:\\caption{Temperature schedule sensitivity at 50\\% sparsity (WikiText-2 PPL$\\downarrow$, TEAL global).}\npaper/appendix.tex:88:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:90:\\caption{Training convergence at 50\\% sparsity (WikiText-2 PPL$\\downarrow$, TEAL global).}\npaper/appendix.tex:116:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:118:\\caption{Penalty controller convergence across sparsity targets (multiplicative schedule, margin $\\epsilon=0.02$, 1000 training steps). Deviation is $|$actual $-$ target$|$ / target.}\npaper/appendix.tex:140:\\caption{Adaptive penalty controller convergence across sparsity targets. Upper panel: penalty weight $\\lambda$ (log scale) over training steps. The 30\\% target requires extreme penalty ($\\lambda \\to 5000$) to suppress activations below their natural density, while 50\\% needs only $\\lambda \\approx 8$ since SwiGLU gates already cluster near this sparsity. Lower panel: actual sparsity (solid) converges to the target (dashed) within $\\sim$10 steps via geometric doubling.}\npaper/appendix.tex:156:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:158:\\caption{Out-of-distribution evaluation on C4 validation (PPL$\\downarrow$). 30\\% KL reports 2-seed mean$\\pm$std; other entries use seed 42.}\npaper/appendix.tex:185:\\caption{Per-layer neuron retention under global top-$k$ allocation at 30\\%, 50\\%, and 70\\% sparsity. Dashed lines indicate uniform retention baselines. Late layers (L24--31) are consistently starved due to long-tailed activation distributions, with retention dropping to 28.4\\% (L31) even at 30\\% sparsity. At 70\\%, starvation extends to mid-layers (L10--12: 16.7--20.1\\%), explaining the transition to the capacity-limited regime.}\npaper/appendix.tex:213:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:215:\\caption{Extended downstream benchmarks at 30\\% sparsity (LLaMA-3.1-8B, seed 42). HellaSwag: 10-shot normalized acc; PIQA: 0-shot normalized acc; BoolQ: 0-shot acc.}\npaper/appendix.tex:241:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:243:\\caption{Qwen-2.5-14B at 30\\% sparsity. \\textbf{Bold}: best sparse per column per group. BCE collapses to 6.4\\% native sparsity (\\S\\ref{sec:method:gradient}), so forced global top-$k$ is used.}\npaper/appendix.tex:270:\\begin{table}[h]\npaper/appendix.tex:272:\\caption{Cross-architecture comparison (WikiText-2 PPL$\\downarrow$). Adv = TEAL\\,/\\,KL ratio. LLaMA uses global top-$k$; Mistral uses uniform allocation (matched within each architecture).}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016svwBNrGVuBL2TXJUFm1dY","session_id":"d20b1017-1e16-43f7-9487-d4eda4cc7ade","uuid":"784fb7ab-aeb0-4bbb-bf80-6ef0e147b929","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:25:33.013Z"}
